## Frequently Asked Questions

### What does a skin lab commercial renovation in Vancouver typically include?
A skin lab renovation in Vancouver focuses on three core areas: clinical workspace optimization (treatment room layout, custom cabinetry for product and equipment storage, sanitation-compliant surface materials), lighting design (medical-grade task lighting in treatment zones, warm ambient lighting in client reception areas), and client-facing aesthetics (reception, waiting area, consultation spaces). The goal is a space that communicates cleanliness and professionalism to clients while maximizing operational efficiency for practitioners.

### What lighting requirements apply to Vancouver skin labs and aesthetics clinics?
Treatment rooms require medical-grade task lighting — typically 2,700–5,000K colour temperature, adjustable intensity, and CRI (colour rendering index) of 90+ to accurately assess skin tone during treatments. Reception and client waiting areas benefit from warmer, 2,700–3,000K ambient lighting to create a welcoming atmosphere. All lighting should be on separate circuits so treatment rooms can be independently controlled. BC Electrical Code compliance and permits are required for lighting circuit additions.

### How much does custom cabinetry add to a Vancouver skin lab renovation?
Custom cabinetry in a Vancouver skin lab renovation typically adds $8,000–$20,000 depending on the number of treatment rooms and storage requirements. Skin labs require cabinetry designed around specific equipment dimensions, product storage volumes, and sanitation requirements — off-the-shelf cabinetry rarely meets operational needs. Custom solutions maximize storage efficiency and create a cohesive professional aesthetic. Cabinet materials must be sealed and non-porous to withstand regular sanitization with clinical-grade products.

### Can a skin lab remain open during renovation in Vancouver?
In most cases, yes — with phased scheduling. Reno Stars planned the Vancouver Skin Lab renovation to allow partial operations to continue throughout. Treatment rooms were renovated sequentially, with the reception and waiting area last, ensuring clients experienced a professional environment at each stage. Full operational pause was limited to 3–5 days during electrical and ventilation work affecting all areas simultaneously. This phased approach minimizes revenue loss during renovation.

### What permits are needed for a skin lab renovation in Vancouver?
A commercial renovation permit from the City of Vancouver is required for any construction, mechanical, electrical, or plumbing work. For health-related facilities, Fraser Health Authority guidelines may apply to specific room configurations (ventilation, hand-washing stations, waste disposal). Sign changes require separate sign permits. The Vancouver commercial permit process for renovation projects of this type typically takes 4–8 weeks. Working with a renovation company experienced in Vancouver commercial permits ensures compliance without surprises.

