## Pre-Renovation Must-Dos for PoCo Sellers

**1. Poly-B inspection:** Port Coquitlam has an extremely high prevalence of Poly-B plumbing (grey plastic pipe) in homes built 1978–1995. This affects insurance, buyer financing, and perceived value. Replacement cost: $8,000–$16,000. ROI is extremely high — a disclosed Poly-B issue costs sellers far more than the replacement cost in price reductions and buyer negotiation leverage.

**2. Electrical panel assessment:** Many Hyde Creek and Woodland Acres homes have original 100-amp panels. Modern household demands (EV charging prep, heat pump, induction range) require 200-amp service. Upgrade before listing: $3,500–$5,500.

**3. Pre-listing inspection:** Commission a home inspection ($450–$650) to identify issues buyers will find — and fix the high-impact ones before listing.

## Timing Your PoCo Pre-Sale Renovation

**Peak listing periods:** March–May (spring market) and September–October (fall).

**Start timeline for spring listing:** Begin renovation in January. 6–8 weeks for kitchen + bathroom + flooring + permit processing.

**Chinese New Year timing:** PoCo''s growing Chinese-Canadian community creates meaningful demand in January–February. A move-in-ready PoCo home listed in late January captures buyers wanting to settle before the New Year.




If you live in a strata property (condo or townhouse), review BC''s [strata renovation rules](/blog/strata-renovation-rules-vancouver/) before starting work — many projects require strata council approval and must follow specific noise, hour, and contractor requirements.
## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the highest-ROI pre-sale renovation for a Port Coquitlam home?
Poly-B pipe replacement and basement suite legalization consistently deliver the best returns in PoCo. Poly-B replacement costs $8,000–$16,000 and adds $18,000–$35,000 in value (ROI 180–220%) — and a disclosed Poly-B issue costs sellers far more than replacement in buyer negotiation leverage. Basement suite legalization runs $18,000–$38,000 but can add $40,000–$85,000 in value (ROI 180–225%) while also qualifying the property for rental income.

### How much does a pre-sale kitchen renovation cost in Port Coquitlam?
A full kitchen renovation in Port Coquitlam runs $28,000–$50,000 and typically adds $45,000–$82,000 in home value — a 160–175% ROI. For Hyde Creek and Woodland Acres homes (1970s–1990s builds), even a focused update (new cabinet doors, countertops, backsplash, ventilation) can deliver strong returns without a full gut renovation. Allow 3–5 weeks for a complete kitchen renovation plus permit processing.

### Does Poly-B pipe replacement increase home sale value in Port Coquitlam?
Yes — significantly. Port Coquitlam has extremely high Poly-B prevalence in homes built 1978–1995 (Hyde Creek, Woodland Acres). Buyers and their lenders specifically flag Poly-B during inspections, and the disclosed presence often triggers $20,000–$40,000 in price reductions or conditions. Full Poly-B replacement at $8,000–$16,000 is nearly always the highest-ROI pre-sale investment in affected properties.

### When should I start renovations for the spring Port Coquitlam market?
Begin your pre-sale renovation in January for a March–May spring listing. Most kitchen + bathroom + flooring packages take 6–8 weeks including permit processing. If Poly-B replacement or electrical panel upgrades are needed, add another 2–3 weeks. Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands. Older Metro Vancouver homes may also need our [Poly-B Pipe Replacement](/en/services/poly-b-replacement/) service to eliminate unsafe plastic piping. Port Coquitlam''s growing Chinese-Canadian community also creates strong pre-Chinese New Year demand — a move-in-ready listing in late January captures that buyer pool.
### What is the ROI on a master ensuite renovation for pre-sale in Port Coquitlam?

A master ensuite renovation in Port Coquitlam delivers strong pre-sale ROI: cost $18,000–$35,000, value added $28,000–$55,000, for a return of 155–165%. Buyers in PoCo''s detached home market respond strongly to renovated ensuites — a tub-to-shower conversion with frameless glass is among the most visible and emotionally compelling upgrades a pre-sale seller can make.

### What is the ROI on LVP flooring replacement for pre-sale in Port Coquitlam?

LVP (luxury vinyl plank) flooring throughout a Port Coquitlam home costs $8,000–$15,000 and adds $12,000–$24,000 in sale value — a 150–160% return. Flooring is also the highest-impact cosmetic improvement per square foot: it affects every room simultaneously, creates immediate visual coherence, and removes the most common buyer objection (worn or dated carpet). Completing flooring before photography maximizes both online click-through and in-person first impressions.



