## Frequently Asked Questions

### Are there renovation grants or rebates available in Vancouver in 2026?

Yes. Key programs available to Metro Vancouver homeowners in 2026: (1) **BC Hydro rebates** — up to $6,000 for heat pump installation, $1,000 for smart thermostats, $200–$400 for insulation upgrades; (2) **FortisBC rebates** — $1,500–$3,000 for heating equipment upgrades; (3) **Canada Greener Homes Grant** — up to $5,600 for eligible energy efficiency retrofits including insulation, windows, and heat pumps (program being wound down — apply before it closes); (4) **City of Vancouver home energy loan** — 0% financing for energy upgrades. Eligibility varies; most require a pre-approved energy audit.

### Can I claim renovation costs on my Canadian taxes?

For most personal-use renovations, no — Canada does not allow a general home renovation tax deduction. Exceptions: (1) **Home Accessibility Tax Credit (HATC)** — up to $3,000 credit (15% of $20,000 expenses) for renovations that improve accessibility for seniors or disabled persons (grab bars, wider doorways, walk-in showers, stair lifts); (2) **Multigenerational Home Renovation Tax Credit** — up to $7,500 credit for creating a secondary suite for a senior or disabled family member; (3) Rental property renovations can be claimed as business expenses on Schedule E if you rent part of your home.

### What is the Multigenerational Home Renovation Tax Credit in Canada?

The Multigenerational Home Renovation Tax Credit (MHRTC) allows Canadian homeowners to claim 15% of up to $50,000 in eligible renovation costs (maximum $7,500 credit) when creating a self-contained secondary dwelling unit for a senior (65+) or a qualifying person with a disability. The unit must have its own entrance, kitchen, bathroom, and sleeping area. The renovation must be completed and the credit claimed in the same tax year. In Metro Vancouver, this effectively provides a $7,500 rebate on a legal [basement suite](/en/guides/basement-suite-cost-vancouver/) conversion — on top of any applicable BC or municipal grants.

### Are there first-time homebuyer renovation programs in BC in 2026?

BC does not have a dedicated first-time homebuyer renovation grant, but first-time buyers may be eligible for: (1) **BC First-Time Home Buyers'' Program** — exemption or reduction in property transfer tax (PTT), freeing up cash for renovations; (2) **RRSP Home Buyers'' Plan** — withdraw up to $35,000 per person from RRSP for purchase + renovation costs; (3) **First Home Savings Account (FHSA)** — up to $40,000 tax-free contributions that can be used for purchase; funds can then free up cash for reno. Always consult a tax advisor for your specific situation.

### How does the Home Accessibility Tax Credit work for bathroom renovations in Vancouver?

The Home Accessibility Tax Credit (HATC) applies to renovations that improve safety or accessibility for seniors (65+) or people with disabilities. For bathroom renovations, eligible expenses include: walk-in shower or tub conversion, grab bar installation, non-slip flooring, wider doorway for wheelchair access, comfort-height toilet, and handheld shower heads. The credit is 15% of up to $20,000 per year ($3,000 maximum credit). The renovation must be at your principal residence and permanently attached to the home. Keep all receipts and a description of how each item improves accessibility.

### What energy efficiency renovation rebates are available for Vancouver BC homeowners in 2026?

BC energy efficiency renovation rebates in 2026: CleanBC Better Homes Program — up to $12,000 for heat pump installation, $4,500 for insulation upgrades, $1,000 for smart thermostats, and other energy retrofit measures. BC Hydro PowerSmart — rebates for EnergySTAR appliances and insulation upgrades in electrically-heated homes. FortisBC Home Efficiency Rebates — for natural gas customers: $1,000–$5,000 for high-efficiency furnace/boiler upgrades, insulation, and air sealing. Canada Greener Homes Grant — federal program offering grants up to $5,600 for eligible energy efficiency upgrades, combined with an interest-free loan up to $40,000. Note: rebate programs change — verify current eligibility at nrcan.gc.ca and bc.ca/CleanBC before planning.

