UPDATE blog_posts SET content_en = '# Bathroom Renovation Port Moody 2026: Complete Cost & Permit Guide

Port Moody is Metro Vancouver''s gem of the Tri-Cities: a compact, walkable city on the shores of Burrard Inlet with a strong sense of community and a housing stock ranging from 1970s waterfront cottages to modern Heritage Mountain townhouses and Rocky Point condos. Bathroom renovations in Port Moody consistently rank among the highest-ROI improvements owners can make — particularly for the city''s large proportion of 1980s and 1990s homes due for their first major refresh.

This guide walks through Port Moody bathroom renovation costs, District of Port Moody permit requirements, neighbourhood-specific considerations, and what to look for when choosing a contractor.

## Bathroom Renovation Costs in Port Moody 2026

### Budget Overview

**Essential Refresh ($16,000–$24,000)**
- Replace toilet, vanity, mirror, lighting
- Re-tile shower or tub surround (same footprint)
- New flooring (LVP or porcelain tile)
- Improved exhaust ventilation
- Fresh paint + trim

**Mid-Range Full Renovation ($25,000–$42,000)**
- Complete gut — down to studs and subfloor
- New walk-in shower or tub/shower combo
- Semi-custom vanity with quartz countertop
- Large-format tile (600×600 mm or larger)
- Electric in-floor heating
- Matte black or brushed nickel fixtures throughout
- Recessed lighting + dimmable controls

**Premium Ensuite ($43,000–$60,000+)**
- Freestanding soaker tub + frameless glass shower
- Double vanity with vessel sinks
- Steam shower option
- Full tile feature wall
- Smart mirror (anti-fog, LED, touch controls)
- Heated towel rail
- Custom niche shelving

### Port Moody Cost Factors

**Rocky Point vs Heritage Mountain pricing**: Heritage Mountain townhouses often have compact ensuite layouts (48–60 sq ft), which limits scope but also cost. Detached homes near Rocky Point or Pleasantside with larger master ensuite footprints (80–120 sq ft) have more scope for premium upgrades — and the market supports the spend.

**Burrard Inlet humidity**: Port Moody''s waterfront microclimate is more humid than inland Tri-Cities neighbourhoods. Bathroom ventilation is critical — budget for a high-CFM exhaust fan ($250–$600 installed) and ensure vapour barrier work is done correctly on exterior walls. Consider our [Heat Pump Installation Vancouver](/en/services/heat-pump-hvac/) service to upgrade heating and cooling with modern energy-efficient systems.

**1980s–1990s housing**: Much of Port Moody was built during this era. Key hidden-cost items to check: Poly-B plumbing supply lines ($2,500–$7,000 to replace in a single bathroom), older 60-amp electrical panels that may need upgrading to support heated floors or additional circuits ($3,500–$7,500 for the whole panel), and fiberglass tub surrounds that were glued directly to drywall (not cement board) — these require more careful demo and moisture remediation before retiling.

## District of Port Moody Permits

### Permit Office

The District of Port Moody Building Department is located at **100 Newport Drive, Port Moody, BC V3H 3E1**. Online permit applications are available through Port Moody''s online portal at portmoody.ca/building.

### When a Permit Is Required

**Required:**
- Adding or relocating any plumbing fixture (toilet, shower drain, tub drain, sink)
- Electrical work beyond a like-for-like fixture swap (e. Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands.g. adding heated floor circuit, upgrading exhaust fan circuit, adding outlets)
- Any structural change (removing non-load-bearing wall to expand bathroom, raising ceilings)
- Converting a tub to a walk-in shower when the drain location changes

**Not required:**
- Replacing a toilet, vanity, or faucet in the same location
- Retiling a shower or floor without moving any plumbing
- Replacing a light fixture with the same wattage rating
- Painting and trim work

### Fee Schedule (District of Port Moody, 2026)

Permit fees are calculated on construction value:
- $0–$5,000: $150 flat fee
- $5,001–$15,000: $150 + $10/thousand over $5K
- $15,001–$50,000: $250 + $8/thousand over $15K
- $50,001–$100,000: $530 + $6/thousand over $50K

Separate mechanical/plumbing permit: $100–$250. Electrical permit (Technical Safety BC): $100–$220.

**Processing time**: District of Port Moody targets 10–15 business days for residential renovation permits. Complex projects (involving structural changes) may take 20–30 business days.

### Strata Properties in Port Moody

Port Moody has a significant proportion of strata-titled townhouses and condos, particularly in Heritage Mountain, Murray-Clarke Connector, and Seaview areas. Before beginning any renovation work on a strata unit:

- Submit a **Renovation Request** to your strata council (minimum 21 days before work starts — check your bylaws, some require 30–60 days)
- Provide your contractor''s WorkSafeBC clearance letter and $2M liability insurance certificate
- Get written approval — verbal approval is not sufficient
- Confirm any restrictions on work hours, elevator use, and waste disposal

District permit and strata approval are both required and completely separate processes.

## Neighbourhood Considerations

### Heritage Mountain

Heritage Mountain''s stacked townhouse complexes were built largely in the late 1980s and early 1990s. Many bathrooms are original — fiberglass tub inserts, low-flow toilets, cultured marble vanity tops. Upgrades to large-format tile, frameless glass, and floating vanities transform these spaces dramatically. Check strata bylaws: many Heritage Mountain complexes restrict work to weekdays and limit noise to 8am–5pm.

### Pleasantside & Murray-Clarke

Pleasantside includes a mix of older single-family homes (1970s ranchers and split-levels) with larger bathroom footprints. These often have original cast iron tubs (worth refinishing rather than demolishing if in good condition — $500–$900 refinish vs. $800–$1,500 to remove and dispose of a cast iron tub). Murray-Clarke condos built in the 2000s are in generally better shape but may have Poly-B supply lines — check under sinks before finalising scope.

### Rocky Point & Inlet District

Properties near Rocky Point Park and the Inlet district include both heritage cottages and modern infill builds. The view premium in this neighbourhood means renovations should aim for premium finishes to match property values. Heritage properties (pre-1950) may have cast iron or galvanised steel drain pipes — budget for drain replacement if you''re opening walls.

### Moody Centre (SkyTrain Area)

The Moody Centre area near the SkyTrain station has seen significant new development — newer condos have more standard bathrooms. Investment properties here benefit from practical, durable renovations (LVP flooring, ceramic tile, semi-custom vanity) rather than luxury finishes, to keep renovation costs proportionate to rental returns.

## Design Trends in Port Moody 2026

**Spa-inspired ensuites**: Port Moody homeowners, many of whom are outdoor recreation enthusiasts (Rocky Point kayaking, cycling, hiking), are drawn to wellness-focused bathroom designs — large rain showers, soaking tubs, heated floors, natural stone-look tile, and greenery (moisture-resistant live plant shelving).

**Frameless glass**: The most requested single upgrade in Port Moody bathrooms. Frameless glass shower enclosures cost $1,800–$4,500 installed and immediately modernise any bathroom.

**Walk-in shower conversions**: Converting a tub/shower combo to a dedicated walk-in shower remains the most popular structural change. Adds significant perceived value in Port Moody''s active-lifestyle demographic.

**Storage solutions**: Hidden storage — recessed medicine cabinets, built-in shower niches, floating vanity with integrated drawer stack — is in high demand in Port Moody''s compact townhouse bathrooms.

