## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does a bathroom refresh cost without full renovation in Vancouver?

See our [verified five-star reviews](/en/reviews/) from Metro Vancouver homeowners.

[About Reno Stars](/en/about/) — Vancouver-based renovation company serving Metro Vancouver since May 2021.

[View our renovation project portfolio →](/en/projects/)

A cosmetic bathroom refresh in Metro Vancouver (without moving plumbing or structural changes) costs $3,000–$12,000 depending on scope. A basic refresh (paint, hardware, mirror, lighting, accessories) runs $3,000–$5,000. Adding a new vanity, toilet, and shower door (same locations) runs $6,000–$10,000. A full cosmetic overhaul including tile surround re-grouting, new fixtures, fresh tile flooring, and new vanity top runs $8,000–$12,000. None of these changes require a permit (no plumbing relocation). Reno Stars provides [free quote](/en/contact/)s for refreshes — we tell you if full renovation would better serve your goals.

### Can I refresh a Vancouver bathroom without replacing tile?

Yes — tile replacement is the most disruptive and expensive part of a bathroom refresh. Alternatives that dramatically change the look without demo: (1) Tile painting with specialty epoxy coating ($800–$2,000 DIY or $2,000–$4,500 professional) — works on ceramic and porcelain; (2) Tile reglazing/refinishing — professional process, $500–$1,500 for a tub surround; (3) New grout ($300–$600) — removes years of staining; (4) Peel-and-stick panels over existing tile — budget option, $400–$900 materials. If the existing tile is cracked or water-damaged behind the surface, replacement is always the right answer — cosmetic fixes won''t stop water damage.

### What gives the biggest visual impact in a bathroom refresh?

The highest visual-impact-per-dollar changes in a Metro Vancouver bathroom refresh: (1) Vanity mirror — a new frameless or framed mirror is $200–$800 and dramatically changes the focal point; (2) Lighting — replacing builder-grade strip lighting with a modern fixture costs $300–$800 installed; (3) Faucet and hardware set — $400–$1,200 for a coordinated brushed nickel or matte black set replaces the most-touched surfaces; (4) Toilet — a comfort-height, elongated toilet runs $600–$1,200 installed and modernizes the most dated element; (5) Shower door — frameless glass instead of a curtain or old frame, $1,200–$2,500, makes the biggest single visual upgrade.

### How long does a bathroom refresh take in Vancouver?

A bathroom refresh with no permit and no tile work takes 3–7 business days depending on scope. Day 1: demo and prep (remove old vanity, toilet, light fixtures). Days 2–3: new vanity, plumbing connections, toilet install, tile painting if applicable. Days 4–5: lighting, mirror, shower door, accessories. Day 5–7: caulking, grouting touch-ups, final clean. Tile floor replacement adds 2–3 days. Custom vanities or special-order items (4–6 week lead time) are the most common cause of delays — Reno Stars orders materials before the project starts to keep the schedule tight.

### Should I refresh or fully renovate my Vancouver bathroom?

Refresh when: budget is under $15,000, layout works, plumbing is in good condition, and you plan to stay in the home 2–5 years before selling. Full renovation when: the layout doesn''t work, plumbing is aging (copper supply lines 30+ years old, galvanized drain lines), tile is cracked or water-damaged behind the surface, or the vanity and tub are more than 20 years old. In Metro Vancouver''s market, a full bathroom renovation ($18,000–$35,000) typically adds $20,000–$40,000 in appraised value — making it financially justified when the refresh can''t fully fix what''s wrong.

### What bathroom updates can Vancouver homeowners do without a contractor or permits?

No-permit, DIY-safe bathroom refresh items in Vancouver: Replace toilet seat, towel bars, toilet paper holder, and hooks — no plumbing or permits. Older Metro Vancouver homes may also need our [Poly-B Pipe Replacement](/en/services/poly-b-replacement/) service to eliminate unsafe plastic piping. Repaint walls (bathroom-rated semi-gloss or satin paint). Replace mirror — no permit if not electrical (no built-in lighting). Regrout tile joints (cleaning, removing old grout, applying new). Replace faucet handle sets — if the valve body stays in place, this is DIY. Refresh caulk around tub and sink — mould removal and recaulk is a high-impact, no-permit refresh. Replace light fixture with same-style and same-wiring configuration — note: BC allows homeowners to do minor electrical work in their own home, but consult an electrician for any new circuit work. Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands.

