## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does an aging-in-place bathroom renovation cost in Vancouver?

An aging-in-place bathroom renovation in Metro Vancouver typically costs $8,000–$35,000 depending on scope. A safety-focused retrofit (grab bars, non-slip flooring, comfort-height toilet, handheld shower) runs $8,000–$15,000. A full walk-in shower conversion with zero-threshold entry, fold-down bench, and barrier-free layout runs $18,000–$35,000. Adding a separate accessible tub costs $12,000–$20,000 more. Note: many of these costs are eligible for the Home Accessibility Tax Credit (HATC) — 15% of up to $20,000 in eligible expenses ($3,000 maximum credit per year).

### What features make a bathroom accessible for seniors in Vancouver?

Key accessible bathroom features recommended by occupational therapists in BC: (1) Zero-threshold (curbless) shower entry — eliminates trip hazard; (2) Grab bars at toilet (both sides), shower entry, and tub (CSA standard locations); (3) Non-slip flooring with coefficient of friction ≥ 0.6 wet; (4) Comfort-height toilet (17–19 inches vs. standard 15 inches); (5) Blocking in walls for future grab bar additions; (6) 36-inch minimum doorway width for wheelchair access; (7) Handheld showerhead on adjustable slide bar; (8) Lever-style faucet handles (not round knobs); (9) Adequate lighting with illuminated switches. Reno Stars works with OT-referred clients across Metro Vancouver.

### Is a walk-in shower better than a walk-in tub for aging in place?

For most Metro Vancouver seniors, a curbless walk-in shower is safer and more practical than a walk-in tub. Walk-in tubs require you to step in, sit down, close the door, then fill — and exit while still wet (the door can''t open until the tub drains). In contrast, a curbless shower allows roll-in wheelchair access and has no water depth to manage. Walk-in tubs cost $8,000–$15,000 installed; a curbless tile shower with bench and grab bars costs $12,000–$25,000 but is universally preferred by care teams. Reno Stars recommends curbless showers for most aging-in-place projects.

### Are grab bars required in new homes in BC?

No — grab bars are not currently required in new single-family homes under the BC Building Code (as of 2026). However, they are required in some multi-unit residential buildings under accessibility provisions. The BC Building Code does require "blocking" (backing) in bathroom walls to allow future grab bar installation in certain multi-unit buildings. For single-family homes, installing proper blocking during any bathroom renovation is best practice — it costs $200–$500 extra and makes future grab bar installation trivial instead of requiring wall demolition.

### Does Canada have grants for aging-in-place home renovations in BC?

Yes — several programs help fund aging-in-place renovations in BC: (1) **Home Accessibility Tax Credit (HATC)** — 15% of up to $20,000 in eligible expenses per year ($3,000 credit); (2) **Canada Mortgage and Housing Corporation (CMHC) Residential Rehabilitation Assistance Program (RRAP)** — loans up to $16,000 forgivable over 5 years for accessibility modifications (income-tested); (3) **BC Housing Home Adaptations for Independence** — up to $20,000 in forgivable loans for seniors and people with disabilities (income-tested); (4) **Veterans Affairs Canada** — equipment and home modification grants for eligible veterans. Reno Stars can provide itemized invoices in the format required for HATC and RRAP claims.

### Can aging-in-place bathroom renovation costs be claimed as a tax credit in BC?

Yes — two federal tax programs cover eligible aging-in-place bathroom renovations: (1) The Home Accessibility Tax Credit (HATC) allows a 15% non-refundable federal tax credit on up to $20,000/year in eligible renovation costs — that''s up to $3,000 back per year. (2) The Renovation for Accessibility (RRAP) program provides forgivable loans for qualifying homeowners. Eligible work includes grab bar installation, walk-in shower conversions, slip-resistant flooring, and widening doorways. Reno Stars provides itemized invoices specifically formatted for HATC and RRAP claims.

