## Frequently Asked Questions

**How much does a kitchen renovation cost in Maple Ridge in 2026?**
Budget kitchen renovations in Maple Ridge: $15,000–$26,000. Mid-range full replacements: $26,000–$43,000. High-end custom: $43,000–$72,000. Older pre-1980 homes may need an additional $1,500–$4,000 electrical panel upgrade.

**How much does a bathroom renovation cost in Maple Ridge in 2026?**
Budget refreshes: $12,000–$20,000. Mid-range full renovations: $20,000–$34,000. High-end primary bathrooms: $34,000–$58,000.

**Do I need a permit for kitchen renovation in Maple Ridge?**
Yes, if you''re moving plumbing, upgrading electrical, or modifying walls. The City of Maple Ridge Building Department processes most residential permits in 2–3 weeks.

**My Maple Ridge home is on a septic system — does that affect my kitchen renovation?**
Yes — if you''re adding fixtures or significantly increasing water usage, consult with the City''s Development Services about septic capacity before planning your renovation. Adding a large kitchen island with a second sink to an older septic system may require a septic assessment and potentially an upgrade.

**Does Reno Stars serve Maple Ridge?**
Yes — we serve all Maple Ridge neighbourhoods including Silver Valley, Albion, Cottonwood, Central Maple Ridge, and Hammond. We''re familiar with the City of Maple Ridge permit process.

**What should I know about renovating an older Maple Ridge home?**
Pre-1980 Maple Ridge homes frequently have 60A or 100A electrical panels — insufficient for a modern kitchen running induction cooktops, dishwashers, and refrigerators simultaneously. Our [Critical Load Panel Installation](/en/services/critical-load-panel/) service upgrades your home electrical capacity for modern renovation demands. Budget $1,500–$4,000 for a panel upgrade to 200A alongside your kitchen renovation. Older homes in Hammond, Ruskin, and central Maple Ridge may also have aging galvanized plumbing that should be assessed before a bathroom renovation. Homes on rural acreage (Cottonwood, Thornhill) are often on septic systems — any fixture additions require a septic capacity check before permitting. Newer Silver Valley and Albion subdivisions (built 2000s–2015) typically have modern 200A service and PEX or copper plumbing, making renovation more straightforward and budget-predictable. Older Metro Vancouver homes may also need our [Poly-B Pipe Replacement](/en/services/poly-b-replacement/) service to eliminate unsafe plastic piping.


