<h2>What&#39;s Included in a Typical Burnaby Bathroom Renovation</h2>
<p>A full bathroom renovation in Burnaby typically includes:</p>
<p><strong>Demolition and prep</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Remove existing tile, vanity, toilet, tub/shower surround</li>
<li>Inspect subfloor and repair if needed</li>
<li>Update waterproofing membrane (required by BC Building Code)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Plumbing work</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Relocate or replace supply and drain lines as required</li>
<li>Install new shower valve, tub filler, or faucet</li>
<li>Rough-in for any relocated fixtures</li>
<li>Pressure test before closing walls</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Tile and waterproofing</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Floor tile installation (heated floor optional)</li>
<li>Shower or tub surround tile (floor-to-ceiling is the standard in Burnaby condos)</li>
<li>Grout sealing</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Fixtures and finishing</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Vanity installation with undermount or integrated sink</li>
<li>Mirror or medicine cabinet</li>
<li>Lighting — potlights, vanity bar, or feature lighting</li>
<li>Towel bars, toilet paper holder, hooks</li>
<li>Toilet installation (optional — most clients keep the existing toilet if it&#39;s in good condition)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Strata Requirements for Burnaby Condos and Townhouses</h2>
<p>Most Burnaby bathroom renovations require strata approval before work begins. Our [cabinet refinishing](/en/services/cabinet/) service can transform your cabinets without full replacement. Here&#39;s what the strata typically requires:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Application form</strong> — description of work, start/end dates, contractor information</li>
<li><strong>Insurance certificate</strong> — contractor liability insurance naming the strata corporation</li>
<li><strong>Noise hours compliance</strong> — in most Burnaby stratas, noisy work is permitted Monday–Friday 8 AM–5 PM only</li>
<li><strong>Elevator booking</strong> — material deliveries and debris removal must use the service elevator</li>
<li><strong>Flooring requirements</strong> — if removing carpet and installing hard flooring, some stratas require a specific STC (sound transmission class) rating for the underlayment</li>
</ol>
<p>We manage all of this as part of our standard service for Burnaby condo projects.</p>
<h2>Neighbourhood-Specific Notes</h2>
<p><strong>Metrotown and Brentwood high-rises:</strong> These buildings typically have strict noise and access schedules. We&#39;re experienced with the logistics of major towers in both areas and pre-coordinate with building management.</p>
<p><strong>Burnaby townhomes (Burnaby Mountain, Sullivan Heights, Edmonds):</strong> More flexibility on scheduling, and often a larger bathroom footprint. These projects tend to have better value-per-dollar outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>South Slope and Deer Lake single-family homes:</strong> Older homes (1960s–1980s) often have original tile and plumbing that needs full replacement. Budget an additional $2,000–$5,000 for unforeseen plumbing updates in homes of this era.</p>
